[Music]  hi everyone now welcome to the new  episode of architecting with google  cloud  today we are going to see that as an  organization goes on an application  modernization journey and especially  using cloud native solutions what are  the challenges and the journey that it  has to  undertake especially if the organization  has got applications that have been  there for a while plus combine that with  their newer applications and provide  this  in a consistent fashion via platform  build on  containerization technologies i am ramen  irani i work as a developer advocate at  google cloud and today i'm pleased to  welcome jeff turnum who's a principal  architect at hcl software welcome jeff  to the show  thank you very much for having me yeah  i'm jeff turnum i've been in software  development now for just about 25 years  the last four and a half of those have  been with hcl  i work on what's called the platform  team we work on  cross-platform cross-portfolio  innovations  and in particular for the last couple  years we've been really focused in on  containerization and kubernetes and our  cloud native journey  thanks jeff so let's get started and  tell us a little bit more about hcl and  the sophie product  sure so hcl has a large and growing  catalog of software we offer software to  our users and business partners that  covers a wide range of areas things like  devops tools security tools digital  solutions customer experience and  marketing  and we probably have a lot of the same  challenges that other software providers  do and that is how do you deal with all  of the various platforms that that your  users want to run on how do you handle  licensing and upgrades in a consistent  way how do you do delivery of software  in a consistent way  so we've been looking to solve these  challenges and a couple years ago we  started to see that cloud native  technologies we're starting to provide a  really nice answer for a lot of those  questions  and so we use that opportunity to take a  fresh look  and we've been on a cloud-native journey  evolving our software portfolio since  then  and i work on what's called sophie short  for solution factory it's basically our  face  to our users and our business partners  of all of our cloud native efforts it's  a catalog that users can browse they can  see all of our cloud native software  and the most exciting part of it is  perhaps anything in the catalog you can  click on and deploy and try out in a few  minutes and then you can also take it  away to your your  cloud provider of choice and install it  there so yeah that's our focus and  that's what we've been working on for  the last couple years that's interesting  so uh you know if we could uh sort of uh  uh go a little bit deeper and maybe you  could tell us about how you sort of went  about your journey and then you know the  role of gcp uh you know towards that  right so getting started on a cloud  native journey is challenging especially  if you're like we were starting from a  position of not being anywhere on that  journey so we had to skill up some teams  we had to understand the landscape of  tools we had to do lots of prototyping  i personally did a tremendous amount of  watching tech talks to see how other  companies that are running their  business on kubernetes are doing these  types of things  and so once we got over that hurdle we  started looking across cloud vendors to  see  you know how are we going to start  building some of these things what  platforms are we going to use and we  pretty quickly realized that gcp and gke  offered a really nice balance of  usability versus full function  and so  all of our development teams that get  started when they try out gcp and in  particular gke the the kubernetes parts  um they get up to speed very quickly  because uh gke makes it very easy to do  things in clusters create clusters  manage clusters and so on and then for  our operations teams that actually run  these things in production  they can get all the capability they  need to go do observability and  monitoring and other things  so for us  we actually did try out several cloud  vendors and we landed back on gcp and  gke because of the ease of use and  because of the power  one of the things we really find  appealing about gcp and gke and this  might sound surprising is the  documentation  so most challenges we have are just the  search away we usually land directly in  the uh gcp dock  and particularly for for  my team what we really like is that  everything that is documented in the uh  as actions you can take in the ui to  implement something usually right there  beside it is documentation on how you  use gcloud or some other tool to  automate that exact same thing and so  for us trying to run a fully deployed  and robust application in kubernetes  automation is key and so we really like  the fact that api's automation is built  in from the ground up um in everything  we do and then also i would also say  um and we weren't expecting this to be  um as as as much of a deciding factor as  it is we really appreciate the gcp  support so opening support tickets and  actually most of our support tickets are  questions we'll ask questions how do we  do this how do we do this particular  thing in kubernetes uh what's what's the  best practice and opening those types of  tickets is easy and getting responses  quickly is also easy so  um yeah across the board we're very  happy with with gcp and gke  thank you jeff for the feedback and it's  it's good to see how our existing online  content helped your team  get up to speed and also the  documentation and support  along with of course the whole  manageability of gke and out of the box  monitoring so i think it's time now to  go to an interesting uh you know part  which is to look at your architecture  and how you sort of brought this into  reality so let's jump into the  architecture  sure so just as a reminder so sophie  short for solution factory provides to  our end users and business partners a  catalog of our software they can browse  it they can learn about everything in  the catalog and then they can deploy it  and they actually have a couple options  when it comes to deployment they can  basically take away a package and go  install it into their own cloud native  environment or what we find gets the  most excitement is anything in our  catalog you can click on and try out in  a few minutes and the way that's done is  we actually do a real  kubernetes deploy when the user says  they want to deploy something so  our architecture works like this when  our users first come to our website they  are hitting a gcp load balancer  that load balancer is protected with  cloud armor so malicious traffic you  know won't even get to our application  uh  typically  so when a traffic does get to our  application then it's hitting what we  call the sophie cluster  which is where our sophie application  runs the application that provides the  catalog and all of the the the web  experience that the users get  so as users browse around the catalog  they're interacting with our our sophie  application and then when a user wants  to deploy something quickly and try it  out in a few minutes  they basically press a deploy button and  that kicks off under the covers a helm  install that will go install what  they've selected out to what we call our  sandbox cluster  this cluster is a very large cluster  that that has all kinds of uh various  node pools in it we have windows node  tools because some of our workloads are  windows we have custom node pools for  some particular workloads that need to  run a certain way and then we have the  generic linux node pools and you know  hundreds of nodes  and so when a user  installs their application gets  installed in this cluster and they get  back uh  network endpoints that they can connect  to a few minutes later typically so  they'll be able to log into our software  try it out and so on  um and  across all of this is all of the images  that are getting pulled all the  container images are getting pulled in  these environments are getting pulled  from  google google's container registry gcr  and for when we want to deliver software  to our end users  we actually use another image registry  for that we're using a harbor very  popular open source image registry and  we run that in yet another gke cluster  and then across all of these  environments and i should say this  environment pictured here  is just one of our environments it's our  production environment we obviously have  internal environments that that for our  dev teams for test teams for performance  for security for staging so we basically  clones of this uh throughout our work  all of these environments are protected  with various levels of security but also  observability and monitoring and  alerting for when things go wrong  and we also have backup done with valero  that  backs up to google storage and we can  restore in the case of emergency very  easily that way so yeah that's basically  the the architecture in a nutshell  thanks jeff for taking us step by step  through the architecture and it's  amazing to see that you know beneath all  that complexity the experience that  you've given to the users is a single  click to deploy the applications what  i'd like to do as you are talking  through  to the architecture is uh specifically  you know  from a gke perspective which forms  really the core of your platform uh  could you tell us specifically a few  things around gk and what particularly  was well suited to your applications  plus i'd like to touch a little bit onto  the whole security aspect with cloud  armor and how that service has also  helped you  sure so yeah when i think about gke the  term that comes to mind is progressive  disclosure and i mean that in the nicest  possible way so when you  as a new user when you try out uh gke  creating your first cluster is just a  few clicks there's a whole bunch of  stuff  beneath the surface there for when you  need it  and so for for us in particular our  operations team needs to understand  at the most detailed level possible  what's going on in our clusters whether  that's the sophie cluster that runs our  application or the sandbox cluster that  runs all of the user deployed  applications or whether that's harbor or  image registry and so what we really  appreciate is that observability and  monitoring and logging is just  built into everything we didn't have to  do anything special to enable it it's  just there waiting for us to run  monitoring and logging queries and get  all that data so we built up an entire  suite around those capabilities where we  have alerts which will kick off when  certain events happen things like a node  going bad or  pods going unhealthy things like that so  we have all kinds of stuff built around  that and i'd say across  you know to your security comment i'd  say across the board our general  experience is when we need to use a gcp  service it's quite straightforward so  you know taking the example of cloud  armor  um i think uh it probably took us an  hour maybe to install cloud armor uh for  our initial prototypes um and that most  of that was just reading the docs to  understand what it was doing the actual  enablement of it with the load balancer  and the appropriate back end in our  cluster was was really really  straightforward  and then we also had to do some testing  to make sure you know um  malicious payloads were getting blocked  and things like that but yeah the the  documentation the defaults they were all  set and tuned very well for our use case  and then when we do need to make changes  as we mentioned earlier it's all in the  docs and and very easy to understand so  i think that's really why we why we like  using gcp so much as the services are  there when we need them and the  capability is there even if it's behind  a very nice  easy to use  facade there's a whole bunch of  interesting stuff waiting to be  discovered  the other interesting thing i'd like to  ask is that as you build this platform  i'm sure you know there were  certain points in time where you had to  make certain architectural decisions or  certain challenges that came your way to  make that platform a reality could you  throw some lights on a few challenges  that you faced and sort of how you had  to overcome it as you went along  sure so yeah because we started from uh  from  ground zero i'm sure we've encountered a  lot of the same challenges that any  other  organization would encounter especially  ones that are starting from an existing  portfolio of software and trying to  modernize it or make it cloud native  so you know we've had to do quite a lot  of internal education and enablement on  these topics and one thing we learned  early on just to get specific one thing  we learned early on was that  it's very easy  to skip over some really important parts  of configuration when you're deploying  kubernetes applications so one example  anyone who's deployed kubernetes apps in  production will probably  understand this quite well but when you  deploy a workload something that's got a  pod  the way you set those  its resource limits basically how much  cpu and memory it can use can have a  dramatic impact on performance um and  and it's it's just two little lines  usually or four little lines in yaml but  it can have a tremendous impact on on  how your application actually runs and  performs and we saw this so we had some  of our uh  existing software that got put into a  cloud-native form factor containerized  packaged it all looked good but when we  ran it  it was actually underperforming it was  actually performing much more slowly  than it would if you deployed onto a vm  or or elsewhere when we looked into it  the root cause was cpu throttling  and so  um the the interesting part of that is  the fix for that is usually very simple  it's tweaking those few lines of yaml  but the trick is knowing what values to  put in in the first place how do i know  how much cpu my workload should request  how do i know what its limit is going to  be  and so we  again opened up a support ticket with  gcp to basically ask that question how  do we set these values how what's the  best practice for for doing this uh the  support folks hooked us up with some  google experts  we got on a call had a chat about this  topic and they basically turned us on to  the power of vertical pod auto scaler  which when you run that tool in  recommendation mode it effectively  answers the question for you it tells  you here's what your cpu setting should  be here's what your memory settings  should be it's basically watching your  application and and and giving you  recommendations so um we've rolled that  out across the the org and we're  now focused on making sure that as our  products change that we keep up to date  with that so we don't want a product  change to introduce something that  requires more cpu and our configuration  doesn't change so it's the question is  how do you monitor how do you make sure  make sure you're continuously uh  checking and improving that so one thing  that we're looking at for the upcoming  year  is if you think back to our architecture  every time a user deploys something to  what we call our sandbox  it deploys to one big cluster that big  sandbox cluster i mentioned and that's  got us it's got us  a lot of benefits and it's got us a long  way  the one nice thing about it is it's one  cluster for our ops team to manage and  maintain and monitor and so when  something goes wrong it's very easy to  know oh there's the cluster that we need  to go fix  and so on  however there are some use cases that we  really want to enable for our end users  and business partners that we can't  really do uh or we can't do it robustly  with this current architecture one of  them is  in addition to just letting users have  access to their endpoints the you know  the the product endpoints that they can  connect to in in the deployed  application we actually want some of our  users to be able to get cube admin  rights to be able to use cubecontrol to  get into their cluster and start poking  around and do things like check on the  status of pods maybe delete some pods  change configuration things like that  that's not something we've been willing  to entertain with a large cluster a  large shared cluster because because of  the security implications and all of the  testing that would be required  so what we're looking at is moving to a  model where  when a user says they want to try out  some of our software we don't just  deploy their application we actually  spin up dynamically  a cluster purpose built for that  workload so it would be sized correctly  it would have the right sort of node  pools you know if it's a windows  workload or it has some windows in there  it would have a windows no pool if not  it wouldn't  and basically spin these things up  quickly on demand  and so we want to move to that sort of  architecture or at least  experiment and make sure that that makes  sense  and basically get to a model of much  more dynamic much more  provisioned infrastructure coming and  going quickly we also want to take a  much closer look at gke autopilot which  lets teams like us  abstract away even further from the  concept of nodes and really just focus  on our pods and workloads  and so we'll be experimenting with that  as well so you have many other examples  but across the board we're always  looking for ways to improve our  architecture so the final thing i wanted  to ask you and i'm just curious about  this is that for a platform of this type  you know there are different  stakeholders in the organization so  could you throw some light on what it  means to be uh you know successful or  how do you measure success for a  platform like this  great question and this is something  we've been uh building up over time over  the last four years that we've been  working on this  and so  the way we see it observability and  improvement happens at various different  various different levels so down at the  technical level uh which we've been  talking about up till now you know for  that you obviously need observability  logging monitoring you need someone  looking at those uh alerts and those  those logs and taking action  so that's that's i'm almost the easy  part because a lot of that uh logging  and monitoring functionality is built in  what's a little more challenging  especially for large organizations with  thousands of developers and you know  dozens and dozens of software products  is how do you move your whole  organization to become cloud native both  in culture and how they deliver things  and so what we've had to do in hcl  is not just hope and pray that  it happens organically we actually at  the executive level have tracking of  what we call our cloud native maturities  we have a cloud-native maturity model  that we've built up um it's basic it's  it's built on a lot of the best  practices that you can go out and find  other people talking about with cloud  native but it's basically a scorecard  and it talks about things like how do  you build your containers both for  security but also for robustness and  logging and and things like that how do  you package your containers into a  kubernetes application effectively um  how do you ensure that the application's  monitorable um how do you ensure  resilience and things like that  so all of our products actually get  scored on that cloud native maturity  model and where there's deficiencies or  gaps that becomes part of the product's  roadmap and so this as i said it's  tracked at the executive level it's not  left as a it's something that each  product manager needs to make sure they  they  carve out time for and so i think in a  large organization that's really the way  it has to be done if your end goal is a  consistent  cloud-native  uh evolution across all of your products  i will mention one other thing just  because it's top of mind we're actually  doing this right now  we're really looking now at a level of  testing that we've never done before i  know other organizations are far ahead  of us on this but we're really looking  at chaos engineering  is a way to make sure that our  applications are resilient against all  kinds of things that can and do go wrong  in production  so we are now setting up  full chaos engineering environments  where we can run all of our products we  can inject we're actually using litmus  chaos open source project for this where  we eject all kinds of bad things  into our cluster things like network  slowdowns cpu memory throttling nodes  going bad pods getting restarted all  kinds of this types of of chaos and  making sure our products can handle it  and recover and in in the best case have  no impact to user experience during  these events yeah it's a it's a constant  journey and if you ask me you know four  or five years from now i'm sure we'll  still be on this journey we'll be  thinking about new things and new ways  to optimize and improve so  it's never ending uh so thank you once  again for sharing uh you know your  journey as well as you know  giving us an overview of how you built  out sophie and particularly the whole uh  you know journey towards cloud native  and ensuring uh you know that along with  gcp you're able to build out this  platform thanks for sharing all your  inputs along the way uh it was a  pleasure to have you on this show thank  you so much jeff yes thanks thanks so  much for having me thank you for joining  us for this episode of architecting with  google cloud if you've enjoyed this  episode please  like and subscribe to this channel thank  you for joining us today  [Music]  you

